course: politics
lesson: 35
title: How to check: who, how, who else
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most claims can be checked with three questions: who says so, how do they know, and who else reports it?
explain: It is like a playground rumour that "school's closed tomorrow": you ask who said it ("a boy in Year 6"), how he knows ("his cousin heard"), and whether any teacher or the school website says the same; usually the rumour falls apart by the second question.
explain: Fact-checkers do this for a living; they also use tricks like lateral reading, searching for a website's name in a new tab, and reverse image search, searching with a picture instead of words.
explain: Checking works both ways: it can show a thing is fake, and it can show a thing is real, like a protest photo printed by three big outlets with the photographer's name.
explain: When something scares you or you cannot tell, show a trusted grown-up and wait before sharing; "not sure yet" is an honest answer.
fact: The first question is who says it; something heard from someone who heard it from someone else is called hearsay.
fact: The second question is how they know: a real study has a name, scientists and usually a link, and other scientists often check it first in peer review.
fact: The third question is who else reports it: real big news is usually reported by many outlets within hours, though sometimes one outlet really gets a true scoop first.
fact: Fact-checkers check claims, look for evidence, ask experts and show their working, and rate claims true, false or misleading.
fact: Lateral reading means checking a website by opening a new tab and reading what others say about it.
fact: A reverse image search finds where else a picture has appeared and when, which can show it is old or edited.
fact: When something online is scary or upsetting, showing a trusted adult is a good step, and it is fine to say "I don't know if this is true".
fact: Breaking news is often wrong at first, so waiting before sharing gives time for the facts to come out.
fact: Sponsored content is an advert made to look like news, and the label "paid for by" tells you someone is selling something.
fact: Checking can also show something is real: a named photographer and several big outlets are good signs.
fact: Some people say to trust official sources first, others say officials can be wrong too, so check the evidence as well.
fact: Words in this lesson: "legit" means real; "bussin'" means really good; "howay" in the North East of England means come on and "bairns" means children; "lekker" in South Africa means great.
exercise: Jake hears that a new law will ban homework. What should he ask first? | Who said that? | How many likes does it have? | Is it funny or not? | What colour was the post?
exercise: Something you heard from someone who heard it from someone else is called... | Hearsay | A news scoop | Peer review | A correction
exercise: A post says "A new study proves screens make kids smarter" with no link. What is the problem? | You cannot find or check the study | Studies are never about screens | It must be true because it says "study" | Posts with no links are always true
exercise: What is peer review? | Other scientists checking a study before it is published | Friends checking your homework for fun | A vote by the people in a class | A newspaper correction of a mistake
exercise: Noah finds a big story on one website only, and no other outlet has it. What should he do? | Wait and see if other outlets report it | Share it quickly so he is first | Believe it, because only one site is brave enough | Forget about checking and decide it is true
exercise: What does a fact-checker do? | Checks claims, looks for evidence and shows how they decided | Writes jokes that copy the style of news, so that people laugh and then look up the facts | Counts votes on election day | Chooses which videos go in your feed
exercise: What is lateral reading? | Opening a new tab to see what others say about a website | Reading a page from right to left | Reading only the about page of a website, where it says who runs it and why | Reading the same article twice
exercise: A shark is swimming on a flooded motorway. How can a reverse image search help? | It can find the same photo from years earlier with no shark | It makes the shark bigger and clearer, so you can see if its teeth are real | It tells you the shark's name | It sends the photo to the police
exercise: Grace sees a scary post about a war and feels upset. What is a good first step? | Show a trusted grown-up before doing anything | Share it with all her friends right away | Keep it secret and worry alone | Reply to the post with angry comments
exercise: Ethan nearly shares a post saying a singer has died. Why is waiting a good idea? | Breaking news is often wrong at first | Waiting makes the post disappear | Singers always post every ten minutes | Sharing late is against the rules
exercise: An article praises a product, and at the bottom it says "paid for by" the company that sells it. What is it? | Sponsored content, an advert dressed as news | A fact-check from a fact-checker | A correction from the newspaper | Independent research by scientists
exercise: What are Finn's three checking questions? | Who, how, who else | When, where and why | Likes, shares, comments | Font, colour and size
exercise: Amira's friend says a protest photo is fake because "it looks weird". Amira finds it in three big outlets with the photographer's name. What does that show? | Checking can show a thing is real, not only fake | Three outlets prove it is three fakes, all copied from each other | A photographer's name is a sign of a fake | Weird-looking photos are always fake
exercise: Why do some people say to check officials too? | Officials can also be wrong or hide things | Officials are never right about anything | Officials do not have websites | Officials are always the same as rumours, so it is best to ignore them
exercise: What is a scoop? | A true story one outlet gets first | A made-up story on a fake news website | A joke headline from a satire website | A short clip cut out of a long speech
test: "legit, a boy in Year 6 said homework's banned by law" — what is the weak part? | We do not know who first said it or how they know | Year 6 boys always know about laws | Nothing, it is clearly true | The law is too short to check
test: Finn's grandad: "I saw it on the internet, so it must be true." What is the best reply? | Let's check who said it, how they know and who else reports it | You are right, the internet is always true | The internet is always false, so ignore anything you read on it, even the weather | Only true things are allowed on the internet
test: In Newcastle: "Howay man, there's a study says screens make bairns clever, it's bussin'!" No link is given. What should you ask? | How do they know: where is the study? | How many friends liked it? | What is the scientist's favourite colour? | Why are bairns using screens?
test: What would happen if a big story were real? | Many outlets would usually report it within hours | Only one strange website would ever have it | Nobody would report it for years | It would only appear in comments
test: A girl says, "If only one news site has a story, it's 100% fake." What is the better version? | It might be a real scoop, so wait before deciding | She is right, single-site stories are always fake | One site is enough to prove anything | Stories are only real if they are on one site
test: In Cape Town: "Lekker, this site says the new phone is the best ever!" At the bottom: "paid for by" the phone company. What should he notice? | It is an advert dressed as news | It is an independent test by scientists | It is a fact-check of the new phone | It is a government warning about phones
test: You don't know a website. What is the smartest move? | Search for the website's name in a new tab | Only read the website's own about page | Believe it if it looks smart | Give it your email to learn more
test: In a cartoon, a detective asks three questions: who saw it, how do they know, and did anyone else see it? Which real skill is that? | Checking a claim before believing it | Making a deepfake | Writing clickbait | Starting a rumour
test: "the war pic's well scary, gonna send it to everyone innit" — what is a better step? | Show a trusted adult first and check before sharing | Send it to everyone as fast as possible, so that they can all be ready | Post it with a scary caption | Hide it and never speak about it
test: A friend says, "That protest pic looks weird, defo fake." Three big outlets have it with the photographer's name. What is more likely? | The photo is real | The photo is fake because it looks weird | All three outlets made the same fake | Photographers' names mean a photo is edited
test: "omg the singer's dead, sharing now!!" Ten minutes later he posts a video. What was the lesson? | Wait before sharing, because first reports are often wrong | Always share first, check never | Singers fake their deaths to get likes, so never believe any news about a singer at all | Videos of singers are always fake
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises "lateral reading"? | The assessment of a source by consultation of what other sources state concerning it | The reading of a document in an unconventional direction | The exclusive consultation of the source's description of itself, as published upon its own pages | The repeated perusal of a single document
exam: Identify the three questions which constitute the checklist recommended in this lesson. | Who asserts it, by what means it is known, and who else reports it | When it was published, where, and for what reason | How many approvals, shares and comments it has received from the public | Which typeface, colour and dimensions it employs
exam: It is given that an article commending a product bears the notice "paid for by" its manufacturer. State the term for such material. | Sponsored content | Peer review | A scoop | A correction
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a reverse image search. | It discloses where and when an image has previously appeared | It renders an image of higher quality, so that any alteration becomes visible to the eye | It identifies the creatures depicted by name | It transmits the image to the authorities
